digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate porous melt-quenched ZIF-type MOF glasses and free-standing membranes for gas separations by using bulky amide solvent SDAs to build large-pore 12-membered-ring topologies and mixed Im/bIm ligands to suppress dense ZIF-zni recrystallization so that porosity and membrane processability are retained.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A practical MOF-glass membrane design must jointly address gas-separation motivation, processable grain-boundary-free form, high accessible porosity, and suppression of dense-phase recrystallization."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Since MOF glasses open opportunities for macroscale, grain-boundary-free morphologies and some ZIFs can form glasses by melt-quenching, melt-quenched ZIF glasses are a plausible platform for producing processable, grain-boundary-free MOF forms."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) For CAN-[Zn(Im)2] and AFI-[Zn(Im)2], recrystallization to dense ZIF-zni before melting is a problem because that recrystallization reduces the porosity of the resultant melt-quenched glass."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Combining bulky amide SDA-directed porous topologies with mixed Im/bIm ligands is supported as a strategy to obtain porous ZIF precursors whose recrystallization to dense ZIF-zni is suppressed and whose porosity is better preserved."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Bulky amide solvents such as DBF/DPF are supported as structure-directing agents for producing large-pore, 12-membered-ring ZIF topologies with imidazole ligands."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because greenhouse-gas mitigation motivates CO2 capture research and crystalline MOFs are potentially applicable in gas separations such as natural-gas purification and CO2 capture, investigating improved MOF-based gas-separation materials is a plausible research direction."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The limited mechanical stability of microcrystalline MOF powders motivates more mechanically stable, macroscale, defect-minimized MOF material forms for membrane devices."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because MOF glass formation reduces precursor porosity and gas-separation performance requires sufficient accessible porosity, practical MOF-glass membranes require strategies that preserve or enhance accessible porosity during or after vitrification."];
  "nI_design_space" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given composition governs melting vs decomposition and the heuristic keeps meltable-like chemistry while increasing topology porosity, therefore one can target crystalline precursors that are both meltable and more porous to yield more porous glasses after quenching."];
  "nI_mem_need" [label="(0,0,0) If gas-separation devices (especially membranes) must be robust, macroscale, and defect-minimized, then the material form must be mechanically stable and preferably grain-boundary-free."];
  "nI_perf_depends_poro" [label="(0,0,0) If a gas-separation material relies on adsorption/molecular sieving, then sufficient accessible porosity (volume/connectivity/appropriate apertures) is required for high capacity/permeance and competitive performance."];
  "nS12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, upon glass formation the porosity of crystalline precursors is reduced, which is unfavorable for gas-separation applications."];
  "nS16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, chemical composition (metal nodes and organic ligands) decisively influences melting point and decomposition temperature in crystalline MOFs."];
  "nS17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, to obtain both meltable and highly porous crystalline MOFs, a proposed heuristic is to engineer a more porous topology while keeping metal nodes/ligands similar to known meltable MOFs (e.g., ZIF-4, ZIF-62)."];
  "nS2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, extensive research targets clean energy production and carbon capture to reduce greenhouse gases (mainly CO2) emitted during energy extraction/production/use."];
  "nS20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, large solvent molecules can act as structure-directing agents (SDAs) to synthesize highly porous MOF topologies without introducing extra ligands."];
  "nS21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, Shi et al. used bulky amide solvents (DBF/DPF) as SDAs to synthesize large-pore ZIFs with Im ligands."];
  "nS24" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, CAN-[Zn(Im)2] and AFI-[Zn(Im)2] recrystallize to dense ZIF-zni (4 MR) before melting (similar to ZIF-4)."];
  "nS25" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, this recrystallization reduces the porosity of the resultant melt-quenched glass (ag ZIF-zni)."];
  "nS27" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, bulky amide solvents used as SDAs create highly porous topologies without introducing extra ligands."];
  "nS28" [label="(28,0,0) Currently, mixed Im/bIm ligands suppress recrystallization to dense ZIF-zni during melting and provide spatial support to preserve porosity."];
  "nS4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, crystalline MOFs have high porosity and precise pore-size distributions, so they are potentially applicable in gas separations (e.g., natural gas purification and CO2 capture)."];
  "nS6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, practical implementation of crystalline MOFs is hampered by limited mechanical stability of microcrystalline powders."];
  "nS7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, the discovery of MOF glasses opens opportunities to process MOFs into macroscale, grain-boundary-free morphologies."];
  "nS9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, some ZIFs (a MOF subclass) can form glasses via melt-quenching."];
  "nS17" -> "nI_design_space" [label="deduction-case"];
  "nS16" -> "nI_design_space" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "nS21" -> "R3" [label="induction-case"];
  "nS20" -> "R3" [label="induction-common"];
  "nS2" -> "R7" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
